## SteamGridDB theme artwork

`search_theme_artwork(query, slot)` accepts `logo` or `hero` and returns SteamGridDB hits with `width` and `height`. Theme Studio also uses the non-SteamGridDB artwork search for backgrounds. `fetch_theme_artwork(url, slot)` accepts SteamGridDB for logos and adds TheGamesDB, RAWG, and Wikimedia CDNs for backgrounds. It requires HTTPS, disallows redirects, caps downloads at 25 MB, and bounds decoded dimensions and memory.

Complexity: one bounded exact-title lookup plus one artwork lookup per slot; filtering is O(n) and score ordering O(n log n) for a provider page, with at most 48 returned hits. Only selected assets are downloaded and decoded. No network or decode work runs for each library tile.


The Steam install command accepts a canonical positive numeric App ID. It checks
the matching Steam Store game record for an advertised Mac build before opening
the Steam client. Steam handles entitlement, compatibility, confirmation, and the
download. Orbit does not edit store download state.

`src-tauri/crates/orbit-motion` prepares RGB lighting samples from a bounded raster decode on a blocking worker. `get_hero_lighting` accepts a game ID and hero or cover slot. It resolves only inline raster data or canonical files under Orbit’s artwork cache and runs at most one decode at a time. It returns three bytes and does not make network requests. The frontend animates layer opacity without frame-by-frame IPC.


`get_glass_material()` returns a cached 64×64 raster texture as a PNG data URL,
a toolbar blur of 12px, and a panel blur of 8px. It accepts no input, reads no files,
and uses a blocking worker. Rendering is O(4096) once per process, independent of
panel size or library size. The frontend fetches the material once per session.


## Native image preparation

`src-tauri/crates/orbit-images` owns image bounds, logo alpha cropping, aspect
ratios, and GPU resizing. wgpu uses Metal, DX12, or Vulkan. Unsupported adapters,
oversized GPU textures, and failed readback use the CPU resize path. Decoding,
encoding, and GPU readback all run on a blocking worker, with one batch active at
a time. A bounded 16-tap shader handles downsampling and transparent edges.

`prepare_theme_previews(onPreview)` accepts a Tauri channel, resolves only the
eight known bundled themes, canonicalizes source paths, rejects symlinks outside
the resource root, limits compressed inputs to 25 MB, and bounds decoded memory.
It streams one result per theme. The channel carries small file paths, while
cached preview bytes stay in Application Support. Cache identity includes source
size and modification time. A warm cache skips source decoding and GPU setup.

Heroes are cropped to 2:1 and prepared at 640×320. Logos trim transparent margins
and fit within 480×320 without upscaling. JPEG heroes and transparent PNG logos
are atomically cached. Production work is O(source pixels) once per changed
image, then O(1) metadata checks per cached slot; the batch has exactly 16 slots.

Optimize the complete packaged artwork from the repository root with `yarn optimize:themes`. It stages display-sized logos and wallpapers with `orbit-images`, generates GPU previews, then encodes WebP variants with `cwebp`. The application does not depend on this build-time encoder.


Theme artwork downloads are optimized and cached on a blocking worker. Custom
theme saves normalize inline images and canonical files under Orbit’s artwork
cache, then save portable image data. External paths are never read by this
normalization step. Dimension caps reduce logo decode memory; wallpapers keep
their aspect ratio. Existing compact JPEG/WebP bytes and bundled references
survive repeated saves without lossy re-encoding. Legacy embedded GIFs retain
their existing behavior.

Packaged WebP previews take a direct fast path: native theme browsing returns the already optimized files without initializing a GPU, decoding full artwork, or generating a second thumbnail cache. The worker/GPU path remains available for bundles lacking prebuilt previews.

Custom theme catalog loading (`services/theme_catalog.rs`) runs file reads, JSON
parsing, image materialization, and thumbnail preparation on a blocking worker.
`list_custom_themes` keeps its array shape and adds a runtime-only `preview` object
with `themeId`, `logo`, and `hero` paths. Branding contains cached display paths or
opaque bundle references rather than inline blobs. The original portable files
are not rewritten. Warm cache reads do not initialize a renderer or decode images.
Cost is O(total bounded JSON bytes + cold image pixels), plus O(n log n) sorting;
IPC payloads scale with theme metadata, not artwork bytes. The frontend shares
concurrent reads and reloads only on startup or the explicit reload action.
